{"name":"Lost in Space","hex":"#03386a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lost","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of lose"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having wandered from, or unable to find, the way.","In an unknown location; unable to be found."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lost"},{"term":"space","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Unlimited or generalized extent, physical or otherwise.\nThe distance between objects. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The distance between objects. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To roam, walk, wander.","To set some distance apart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/space"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lost in Space","blocks":[{"text":"Lost in Space is an American science fiction television series created and produced by Irwin Allen, which originally aired between September 15, 1965, and March 6, 1968, on CBS. Lightly dramatic, sometimes comedic in tone, the series was inspired by the 1812 Johann David Wyss novel The Swiss Family Robinson. The series follows the adventures of the Robinsons, a pioneering family of space colonists who struggle to survive in the depths of space. The show ran for 84 episodes over three seasons. The first season comprised 29 one-hour episodes, filmed in black and white. The 29th episode, however, had a few minutes of color at the end. Seasons 2 and 3 were shot entirely in color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Lost_in_Space_%281965_TV_series_logo%29.svg/330px-Lost_in_Space_%281965_TV_series_logo%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_in_Space","wikidata":"Q536137"},{"title":"Lost in Space (2018 TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Lost in Space is an American science fiction television series following the adventures of a family of space colonists whose ship veers off course. The series is a reimagining of the 1965 series of the same name, inspired by the 1812 novel The Swiss Family Robinson and the 1962 Gold Key comic book Space Family Robinson, created by Del Connell and artist Dan Spiegle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/ff/Lost_in_Space_2018_series_Logo.jpg/330px-Lost_in_Space_2018_series_Logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_in_Space_(2018_TV_series)","wikidata":"Q25341232"},{"title":"Space","blocks":[{"text":"Space is a three-dimensional continuum containing positions and directions. In classical physics, physical space is often conceived in three linear dimensions. Modern physicists usually consider it, with time, to be part of a boundless four-dimensional continuum known as spacetime. The concept of space is considered to be of fundamental importance to an understanding of the physical universe. However, disagreement continues between philosophers over whether it is itself an entity, a relationship between entities, or part of a conceptual framework."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/3D_coordinate_system.svg/330px-3D_coordinate_system.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space","wikidata":"Q107"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b9/Lost_In_Space_%281965_TV_series_cast_members%29.jpg/330px-Lost_In_Space_%281965_TV_series_cast_members%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b9/Lost_In_Space_%281965_TV_series_cast_members%29.jpg/330px-Lost_In_Space_%281965_TV_series_cast_members%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1967 publicity photo showing cast members Angela Cartwright, Mark Goddard, Marta Kristen, Bob May (Robot), Jonathan Harris, June Lockhart, Guy Williams and Billy Mumy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALost_In_Space_(1965_TV_series_cast_members).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg/500px-RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg/1280px-RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg/500px-RKR_7311_%2852013651960%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Each Jupiter spacecraft carries one of these Chariots for surface transportation on a planetary body.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARKR_7311_(52013651960).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/1280px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg/500px-Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gottfried Leibniz","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz%2C_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/GodfreyKnel